# Implementing CI Pipeline on AWS: CodeBuild + CodePipeline + GitHub

## What I Learned Today

Today was a fully hands-on session. I implemented a **Continuous Integration (CI) pipeline** on AWS using:

- **GitHub** — source code repository
- **AWS CodeBuild** — builds the application and Docker image
- **AWS CodePipeline** — orchestrator that ties everything together

> **Note:** Only the CI part is covered today. The CD (Continuous Delivery) stage will be added in the next session.

---

## Project Structure

The sample application used is a basic **Flask (Python)** app. The repository structure is:

```
day-14/
└── simple-python-app/
    ├── app.py               # Flask hello-world application
    ├── requirements.txt     # Only dependency: flask
    ├── Dockerfile           # Builds and runs the Flask app
    └── buildspec.yml        # AWS CodeBuild specification file
```

### Dockerfile (reference)

```dockerfile
FROM python:3.11-slim
WORKDIR /app
COPY requirements.txt .
RUN pip install -r requirements.txt
COPY . .
EXPOSE 5000
CMD ["python", "app.py"]
```

---

## CI Pipeline Flow

```
Developer pushes code to GitHub
        ↓
AWS CodePipeline detects the change (Source stage)
        ↓
CodePipeline invokes AWS CodeBuild (Build stage)
        ↓
CodeBuild runs buildspec.yml:
- Install Python runtime
- pip install requirements
- Docker login
- Docker build
- Docker push → Docker Hub
        ↓
New Docker image available in Docker Hub registry
```

---

## Step 1 — Create an AWS CodeBuild Project

### 1.1 Basic Configuration

1. Go to AWS Console → search **CodeBuild** → **Create build project**.
2. Provide:
- **Project name:** `sample-python-flask-service`
- **Description:** `A sample Python Flask service`

### 1.2 Source — Connect GitHub

1. Under **Source provider**, select **GitHub**.
2. Click **Connect with OAuth** → **Connect to GitHub** → authorize in the pop-up.
3. Select **Public repository** (if using a shared repo) or **Repository in my GitHub account** (for your own repo).
4. Enter the repository URL/name.

### 1.3 Environment

| Setting | Value |
|---|---|
| Operating System | Ubuntu |
| Runtime | Standard |
| Image | Latest available |
| Environment type | Linux |
| Service role | Create new / use existing (see below) |

> ⚠️ **Privileged mode** — must be **enabled** to allow Docker image builds inside CodeBuild. Go to **Edit → Environment → Enable privileged flag**. Without this, Docker daemon commands will fail.

### 1.4 Create a Service Role for CodeBuild

CodeBuild is an AWS service, not a user — so it needs an **IAM Role** (not an IAM User) to interact with other AWS services.

1. Go to **IAM → Roles → Create role**.
2. Select **AWS service** → search for and select **CodeBuild**.
3. Click **Next** (attach permissions later as needed).
4. Name the role: `codebuild-service-role`.
5. Create the role and attach it to the CodeBuild project.

---

## Step 2 — Store Sensitive Credentials in AWS Systems Manager (Parameter Store)

Never hardcode Docker credentials in the buildspec file. Store them securely in **SSM Parameter Store**.

### 2.1 Create Parameters

1. Go to AWS Console → **Systems Manager → Parameter Store → Create parameter**.
2. Create the following three parameters:

| Name | Type | Value |
|---|---|---|
| `/my-app/docker-credentials/username` | SecureString | Your Docker Hub username |
| `/my-app/docker-credentials/password` | SecureString | Your Docker Hub password |
| `/my-app/docker-credentials/registry-url` | SecureString | `docker.io` |

> Use **SecureString** with default KMS encryption for all sensitive values.
> Follow a consistent naming convention: `/<app-name>/<credential-type>/<key>` — this makes it easy to manage hundreds of secrets across multiple services.

### 2.2 Grant CodeBuild Access to SSM

The CodeBuild service role needs permission to read from Parameter Store:

1. Go to **IAM → Roles** → open the CodeBuild service role.
2. **Add permissions → Attach policies** → search for `AmazonSSMFullAccess` → attach.

---

## Step 3 — Write the buildspec.yml

The `buildspec.yml` is the CodeBuild equivalent of a Jenkinsfile. It defines all CI stages.

```yaml
version: 0.2

env:
  parameter-store:
    DOCKER_REGISTRY_USERNAME: /my-app/docker-credentials/username
    DOCKER_REGISTRY_PASSWORD: /my-app/docker-credentials/password
    DOCKER_REGISTRY_URL: /my-app/docker-credentials/registry-url

phases:
  install:
    runtime-versions:
      python: 3.11

  pre_build:
    commands:
- pip install -r day-14/simple-python-app/requirements.txt
- echo Logging into Docker Hub...
- echo $DOCKER_REGISTRY_PASSWORD | docker login -u $DOCKER_REGISTRY_USERNAME --password-stdin $DOCKER_REGISTRY_URL

  build:
    commands:
- cd day-14/simple-python-app
- echo Building Docker image...
- docker build -t $DOCKER_REGISTRY_URL/$DOCKER_REGISTRY_USERNAME/simple-python-flask-app:latest .
- docker push $DOCKER_REGISTRY_URL/$DOCKER_REGISTRY_USERNAME/simple-python-flask-app:latest

  post_build:
    commands:
- echo Build successful!
```

> **Key points:**
> - The `parameter-store` block under `env` pulls credentials from SSM automatically.
> - The `docker login` command must come **before** `docker build` and `docker push` — missing this causes an "access denied" error.
> - The trailing `.` in `docker build -t <tag> .` is required — it tells Docker where the Dockerfile is located.
> - Adjust directory paths (`day-14/simple-python-app/`) to match your own repo structure.

---

## Step 4 — Test the CodeBuild Project (Manual Run)

1. Go to the CodeBuild project → click **Start build**.
2. Monitor the build logs in real time.

### Common Errors & Fixes

| Error | Cause | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| `No such file or directory: requirements.txt` | Wrong directory path in buildspec | Verify and correct the path to `requirements.txt` |
| `Docker build requires exactly one argument` | Missing `.` at end of `docker build` command | Add `.` at the end: `docker build -t <tag> .` |
| `Cannot connect to the Docker daemon` | Privileged mode not enabled | Edit CodeBuild project → Environment → enable **Privileged** |
| `Requested access to the resource is denied` (Docker push) | Missing `docker login` step | Add `docker login` command in `pre_build` phase |
| SSM access denied | CodeBuild role missing SSM permissions | Attach `AmazonSSMFullAccess` to the CodeBuild IAM role |

---

## Step 5 — Create the AWS CodePipeline

Once CodeBuild is working manually, wire it into CodePipeline so it triggers automatically on every GitHub push.

### 5.1 Create the Pipeline

1. Go to **CodePipeline → Create pipeline**.
2. Provide a name: `sample-python-app-pipeline`.
3. Create or use an existing service role.

### 5.2 Source Stage — GitHub

1. **Source provider:** GitHub (Version 2)
2. Click **Connect to GitHub** → provide a connection name → authorize.
3. Select **Repository:** your GitHub repo
4. Select **Branch:** `main`
5. Output artifact format: default (CodePipeline default)

### 5.3 Build Stage — CodeBuild

1. **Build provider:** AWS CodeBuild
2. **Project name:** select `sample-python-flask-service`
3. Build type: Single build

### 5.4 Deploy Stage

Skip for now — the deploy stage (CodeDeploy) will be configured in the next session.

### 5.5 Review and Create

Review all stages and click **Create pipeline**. The pipeline will run immediately with the current code.

---

## Step 6 — Verify End-to-End Automation

To verify the pipeline triggers automatically:

1. Make any small change to the GitHub repository (e.g., add a blank line to `app.py` and commit).
2. Watch the CodePipeline console — the **Source** stage transitions to **In Progress** immediately.
3. Once Source completes, the **Build** stage triggers automatically.
4. When the build succeeds, verify the new Docker image has been pushed to Docker Hub with an updated timestamp.

---

## How buildspec.yml Compares to Other CI Tools

| CI Tool | Pipeline Definition File |
|---|---|
| Jenkins | `Jenkinsfile` (Groovy) |
| GitHub Actions | `.github/workflows/*.yml` |
| GitLab CI | `.gitlab-ci.yml` |
| AWS CodeBuild | `buildspec.yml` |

All follow the same principle: define stages/phases → run commands in each phase.

---

## Summary

| Component | Role |
|---|---|
| GitHub | Source code repository (triggers the pipeline on push) |
| AWS CodePipeline | Orchestrator — detects source changes and invokes CodeBuild |
| AWS CodeBuild | Executes CI stages defined in `buildspec.yml` |
| SSM Parameter Store | Secure storage for Docker credentials |
| Docker Hub | Target registry for the built Docker image |

---
